TEXT -360 -160 Left 2 ;pure ohmic load
TEXT -560 -112 Left 2 ;The generator has a fixed speed of (1500 turns/min)\nand is connected to a isolated network with no voltage source\nThe field voltage is fixed at 600V \nThe load is zero
TEXT -240 -376 Left 2 ;General:\n \nThis model is a synchrongenerator without transformer, switch or regulator for speed or voltage\nThe speed is fix at (n_Turbine=1500)\nThe field voltage is set by a external PWL-source\n \nparams:\n            UN_verkettet=20000 Nominal voltage\n            IN=14.434k Nominal current\n            Pel=400e6 Nominal power (UN_verkettet*IN*cosphi of 0.8)\n            Kc=0.5 short-circuit ratio\n            UE0=600V Nominal field voltage\n            IE0=2000A Nominal field current\n            p=2 Number of poles      \n            Schenkelpolfactor=0.5 for salient pole machine (kc=0.8-1.0)\n                                              1 for cylindrical pole machine(kc=0.4-0.5)                               \n             Daempferfaktor=1 (damping factor)\n             Streuung=0.05 (leakage factor)\n             rcu=0.01 (rel. Copper resistance)\n             rbg=0.001 (rel. friction moment)\n             J=100000 (inertia)\n             a=0.9 parameter for nonlinear saturation\n             m=7  parameter for nonlinear saturation\n             n1=1500 The beginning speed\n             err0=0  (if err0=1 generator starts with full voltage!, because of spike set transient starting time to 1ms)\n             t_anlauf=2m Starting time\n             t_auslauf=2s Stopping time\n             t_betrieb=100s In-operation time\n             n_Turbine=Nominal speed turbine\n              \n \nAlternative elements: (changed via doubleclick on the Spicemodelline)\n          \nHINT: The power output can be computed with M*314.159/2  (P=M*omega/p)\n                    The load angle can be outputed with V(u1:u1:theta) \n                    The here constant frequency is n/30    2*n/60 = p*n/60 (p=Polcount)\n \nLibfile: Sgen.lib
TEXT -848 -96 Left 2 ;Since the generator acts here with fixed voltage (600V-field voltage)\nthere is no need for voltage regulation\nThe excitation voltage can be altered by means of a pwl source
TEXT -568 -264 Left 2 ;Phase 3 grounded
TEXT -808 -528 Left 2 ;From start on phase 3 is shorted and makes a assymetric current system. \nThis system can be translated to a positive sequence, negative sequence and\nzero sequence system. The negative sequence system (-omega) has double velocity\nin relation to the rotor and induces alternating currents with double frequency in the exciter \ncircuit. This alternating field can now also be divided into two rotating fields one with +omega\nand one with -omega. The field with +omega compensates the conterclockwise stator field above and\nthe other has now 3*omega related to the rotor and induces now stator currents with\nthree times the frequency of the net. You can see this with Edit/FFT (zoom before) especially if you set\nthe damping facotor of the generator to a very small value.\nInstead of a shorted phase you can also simulate asymetric load
TEXT -312 -576 Left 5 ;Try to set the damping factor to 0.001 and 100 and 1\n( See the higher freuqency currents )\nNOTE: If no damping (factor=0.001) the third harmonics\nget very large !!!\nTherfore damping is important for:\na) damping of mechanical oscillation\nb) the better sinus shapes of the voltages and currents\n   filter out 3,5,7,9 harmonics of net frequency
TEXT -808 -576 Left 5 ;Set err0=1 to have full voltage from the begin !!!
TEXT -800 -368 Left 2 ;Start of transient analysis at 1ms since\nat time=0 there is a large spike
RECTANGLE Normal 192 48 -256 -400 2
